2	//
3	// Plain server-side module (NO "use server") — the shared core behind every
4	// "point DNS at this server" button (control-panel Websites, post-migration
5	// domain cutover). Server actions do the auth/ownership/rate-limit work and then
6	// call pointHostnameToIp; this module owns only the DNS mechanics:
7	//
8	//   1. validate the hostname (never trust client input in an API path),
9	//   2. decrypt the customer's stored Cloudflare token,
10	//   3. resolve which of their zones the hostname belongs to,
11	//   4. upsert a single A record hostname -> ip, PROXIED = FALSE.
12	//
13	// Proxied is deliberately false (DNS-only / "grey cloud"): the A record must
14	// resolve to the customer's own server so Let's Encrypt HTTP-01 validation hits
15	// certbot on that box, and so non-HTTP services on the host stay reachable.
16	// Behind Cloudflare's proxy the challenge would be answered by Cloudflare's
17	// edge and issuance would fail.
18	//
19	// The token is never logged, returned, or included in any error message.
